  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The query is a glipizide-like sulfonylurea sharing the N-cyclohexylsulfonylurea core with glyburide; L4 Caco-2 data for glyburide show high absorptive permeability (Papp 18.1 ± 0.8 ×10^-6 cm/s; 24.9 ± 5.7 ×10^-6 cm/s with Pgp inhibition), supporting efficient intestinal absorption for this chemotype despite ionization.",
      "card_ids": ["C170","C125"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Glyburide solubility in fasted human intestinal fluid is 5–66 µg/mL (thermodynamic HIF solubility 9.2–15.4 µg/mL), and glyburide formulations show complete release in FaSSIF, indicating that luminal dissolution is not an inherent barrier to absorption for this sulfonylurea class.",
      "card_ids": ["C135","C129","C157","C145"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Low aqueous solubility values for related sulfonylureas (glibenclamide intrinsic 350 ng/mL; glimepiride 1.6–8 µg/mL in water/buffer) are raw-material/medium-specific and are outweighed by the high permeability and intestinal-fluid solubility; they do not affirm F<20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C158","C164","C146","C170","C135"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L3 oral-exposure records for glyburide, glimepiride, gliclazide, and tolbutamide show substantial systemic AUC/Cmax after oral dosing in humans (e.g., glyburide AUCss 525.7 ng·h/mL; glimepiride AUC0-∞ 1215 ng·h/mL; gliclazide AUC0-∞ 56.32 µg·h/mL), consistent with F≥20% for the sulfonylurea class.",
      "card_ids": ["C97","C84","C108","C121","C86","C81","C87"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "All visible L1 direct F records are ≥20% (esaxerenone 90.8%, benazeprilat 27.1–28.5%, praziquantel 27.2%), so no direct gold record supports F<20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C02","C11","C12"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "intestinal_absorption_permeability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C170","C125","C157","C145","C135","C129","C126","C137","C153"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct absolute oral bioavailability measurement for glipizide or any sulfonylurea is supplied; L4 permeability/solubility records are for glyburide/glimepiride/gliclazide/tolbutamide, not glipizide.",
    "No matched oral-versus-IV comparison for the query or related sulfonylureas is supplied.",
    "No glipizide-specific Caco-2, solubility, or dissolution data are supplied; transfer from glyburide is inferred from the shared sulfonylurea pharmacophore.",
    "L5 gut-wall efflux/intestinal metabolism evidence has not yet been assessed; glyburide shows some Pgp sensitivity (C125), and its impact on glipizide is unknown.",
    "Condition match is imperfect: the query has no reported external condition, while some contexts are fed/fasted/cirrhosis."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted high because the query is a glipizide-like sulfonylurea; L4 shows high Caco-2 permeability and adequate intestinal-fluid solubility/dissolution for the closely related sulfonylurea glyburide, and L3 shows substantial oral exposure for the sulfonylurea class. Low aqueous solubility records are medium-specific and do not affirm F<20%. Confidence remains moderate because no direct F measurement for glipizide is supplied."
}

